      "Switch off, step out: could a forest beat your phone?",
      "Be honest: when did you last go a whole hour without looking at a screen? If you cannot remember, you are not alone. Many of us spend our free time scrolling, watching and replying, and then wonder why we feel tired and restless.",
      "There may be a simple cure. In Japan, people practise shinrin-yoku, often translated as “forest bathing”. It involves no sport, no targets and no phone; you simply walk slowly among the trees and notice the sounds, smells and colours around you. Many people who try it say they feel calmer, and some researchers believe that time among trees may help to lower stress.",
      "Compare that with a typical evening online. Screens give us endless stimulation, yet they rarely let the mind rest, and bright light late at night may make it harder to fall asleep. A forest, by contrast, offers an abundance of gentle stimulation: moving leaves, birdsong, fresh air. Not only does it ask nothing of you, but it also gives your attention a chance to recover.",
      "You do not need a huge woodland, either. A park, a garden or even a tree-lined street will do. Start with fifteen minutes, leave your phone in your pocket and let your mind wander.",
      "So the next time you feel drained, ask yourself: do I need another notification, or do I need some trees? The answer may be waiting just outside your door."
